speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, under the provisions of Standing Order 43, I ask for the unanimous consent of the House to move a motion dealing with a very urgent matter.
Considering that the Outaouais regional school board, regrouping some 20 high schools from Fort-Coulonge to Gatineau, has experienced major problems with its call for tenders to meet its fuel needs in 1979-80; considering that in recent years the same school board always made such an invitation at the same period of the year and always received bids from four large companies; and considering that much to its surprise, only one bid with oil prices 30 per cent higher than last year has been received, I move, seconded by the hon. member for Pontiac-Gatineau-Labelle (Mr. Lefebvre);
That an investigation be launched as soon as possible to consider what appears to be a cartel at the Outaouais school board and other school institutions in western Quebec as regards the limited number of oil companies interested in bidding.

Mr. Speaker, I rise under the provisions of Standing Order 43 on a matter of urgent and pressing necessity.
In view of the fact that the major oil companies have refused to bid on heating oil supplies for the North Shore district Roman Catholic Separate School Board, due to the uncertainty of oil supplies, and considering that the Minister of Energy, Mines and Resources (Mr. Hnatyshyn) has not established the energy supplies allocation board by appointing the people to serve on it, 1 move, seconded by the hon. member for Cochrane (Mr. Penner);
That the Minister of Energy, Mines and Resources have this matter investigated immediately, to determine the heating oil supply situation for this winter, with a view to taking action to ensure that there are adequate heating oil supplies for Canadians this winter.
